cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata in this file."
title: "AuthGuard: bot defence for the ILIAS account-registration form"
type: software
authors:
  - name: "OC Open Consulting SB Srl"
    website: "https://www.oc-group.eu"
    email: "plugins@oc-group.eu"
abstract: >-
  ILIAS plugin that rejects automated submissions of the self-registration
  form and offers the same protection to other plugins that publish forms to
  anonymous visitors, through a documented form protection API. A submission
  has to pass a submit-timing check (less than two seconds after the form was
  shown is refused), a honeypot (a hidden decoy field that only scripts fill)
  and a challenge provider chosen by the administrator. By default (Altcha)
  the challenge is a proof-of-work solved in the background without a click,
  generated and verified on the ILIAS server, and no visitor data leaves the
  installation. Instead the administrator can select an external provider,
  Google reCAPTCHA v2 or v3 (with a configurable score threshold), Cloudflare
  Turnstile, hCaptcha or Friendly Captcha (global or EU endpoint); these send
  data about the visitor to the vendor. Visitors without JavaScript get an
  image CAPTCHA as a fallback. ILIAS core shipped a CAPTCHA on the
  self-registration form up to ILIAS 7 and removed it in ILIAS 8 on the
  grounds that it no longer kept bots out; this plugin restores the protection
  as a user-interface hook plugin, without any change to the ILIAS core. The
  image fallback is image-only and does not meet WCAG 2.1 success criterion
  1.1.1; installations subject to EN 301 549 should provide an alternative
  registration route. For ILIAS 11 (11.0 and later 11.x), PHP 8.3 or 8.4.
version: 11.1.3
date-released: "2026-11-06"
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
